{
  "chapter_id": "CH005",
  "title": "Access, Not Achievement",
  "sections": [
    {
      "section_id": "SEC018",
      "title": "Anointed but Not Activated",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "You are anointed. That is settled. But being anointed does not by itself guarantee access to what the anointing carries. The anointing must be activated, and that activation comes through a right understanding of what has already been given, not through striving to obtain something God has withheld.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "IDEA060",
            "SRC001716",
            "SRC001720",
            "SRC001721"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC001716",
            "SRC001720",
            "SRC001721"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA060"
          ],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000001",
          "provider_handle": "p1"
        },
        {
          "text": "God has said, I have blessed you. The blessing is not a future promise waiting on performance; it is already spoken over you. The failure, when it comes, is not that God is holding back. It is that we have not learned to access the blessing that is already ours.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "IDEA061",
            "SRC001743",
            "SRC001745",
            "SRC001747"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC001743",
            "SRC001745",
            "SRC001747"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA061"
          ],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000002",
          "provider_handle": "p2"
        },
        {
          "text": "This raises the question of religious disciplines themselves, and how they relate to the God they are meant to serve.",
          "kind": "connective",
          "evidence_handles": [],
          "source_refs": [],
          "idea_refs": [],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000003",
          "provider_handle": "p3"
        },
        {
          "text": "There is nothing wrong with fasting, sacrifice, and other disciplines in themselves. But they become harmful the moment they take the place of God. When a person is fasting, God should still be present at the center of it, not pushed to the periphery by the discipline itself. The danger is not the practice; the danger is letting the practice replace the One it was meant to reach.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "IDEA062",
            "SRC001840",
            "SRC001844",
            "SRC001845"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC001840",
            "SRC001844",
            "SRC001845"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA062"
          ],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000004",
          "provider_handle": "p4"
        },
        {
          "text": "I have a friend, a man of God who was struggling, and God sent him to sit with an elder minister. The elder fed him and otherwise paid no attention to his need for some direct word or instruction. He came there expecting something specific, and for a time there seemed to be nothing. The man began to laugh at this point, realizing that God's own simple presence, without an elaborate pronouncement, was the answer all along.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "EX008",
            "IDEA062",
            "SRC001724",
            "SRC001760",
            "SRC001841",
            "SRC001830"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC001724",
            "SRC001760",
            "SRC001841",
            "SRC001830",
            "SRC001840",
            "SRC001844",
            "SRC001845"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA062"
          ],
          "example_refs": [
            "EX008"
          ],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [
            "UNC007"
          ],
          "paragraph_id": "P000005",
          "provider_handle": "p5"
        }
      ],
      "idea_refs": [
        "IDEA060",
        "IDEA061",
        "IDEA062"
      ],
      "source_refs": [
        "SRC001716",
        "SRC001720",
        "SRC001721",
        "SRC001743",
        "SRC001745",
        "SRC001747",
        "SRC001840",
        "SRC001844",
        "SRC001845",
        "SRC001724",
        "SRC001760",
        "SRC001841",
        "SRC001830"
      ]
    },
    {
      "section_id": "SEC019",
      "title": "The Heart Behind the Offering",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "The attitude behind an offering matters more to God than the offering itself. He receives only what comes from the heart. An unwilling gift, however large, or a joyless sacrifice, however costly, can be nullified by the spirit in which it was given. This is the instruction found in 2 Corinthians chapter 9.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "IDEA063",
            "REF014",
            "SRC001864",
            "SRC001871",
            "SRC001869"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC001864",
            "SRC001871",
            "SRC001869",
            "SRC001881"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA063"
          ],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [
            "REF014"
          ],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000006",
          "provider_handle": "p6"
        },
        {
          "text": "If the sacrifice itself becomes more important to you than God, then that sacrifice is cancelled. It is not God who cancels it; it is your own misplaced priority that cancels it. The gift was meant to lead to him, and when it instead becomes the object of devotion in his place, it defeats its own purpose.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "IDEA064",
            "SRC001877",
            "SRC001881",
            "SRC001888",
            "SRC001890"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC001877",
            "SRC001881",
            "SRC001888",
            "SRC001890"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA064"
          ],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000007",
          "provider_handle": "p7"
        },
        {
          "text": "Ecclesiastes chapter 5, verse 4, speaks in the same vein regarding vows made to God.",
          "kind": "connective",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "REF048",
            "SRC006544",
            "SRC006546"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC006544",
            "SRC006546"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [
            "REF048"
          ],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000008",
          "provider_handle": "p8"
        }
      ],
      "idea_refs": [
        "IDEA063",
        "IDEA064"
      ],
      "source_refs": [
        "SRC001864",
        "SRC001871",
        "SRC001869",
        "SRC001881",
        "SRC001877",
        "SRC001888",
        "SRC001890",
        "SRC006544",
        "SRC006546"
      ]
    },
    {
      "section_id": "SEC020",
      "title": "Worship as Fellowship",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Sometimes we worship our own singing. We pay attention to how the words are said, to the solemnity of the moment, to the performance of the thing, rather than to the God the worship is addressed to. Even lifting up hands can become an outward gesture emptied of the encounter it was meant to express, done because it is expected rather than because it rises naturally from an experience of his presence.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "IDEA065",
            "SRC001921",
            "SRC001926",
            "SRC001930"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC001921",
            "SRC001926",
            "SRC001930"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA065"
          ],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000009",
          "provider_handle": "p9"
        },
        {
          "text": "When a person genuinely finds God, nobody needs to tell them to lift up their hands. There is so much liberty in that encounter that self-consciousness falls away entirely. The response is spontaneous, not instructed, because it flows directly out of having actually met him.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "IDEA066",
            "SRC001932",
            "SRC001937",
            "SRC001945"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC001932",
            "SRC001937",
            "SRC001945"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA066"
          ],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000010",
          "provider_handle": "p10"
        },
        {
          "text": "In November 1986 I found myself, during a reading of Scripture, drawn into Isaiah 42 as though swallowed up in it — I reasoned only Isaiah 42, absorbed in it in a way I can only describe as being imbibed in the text. A prophet to whom this was later mentioned dismissed it rather casually, simply saying, that's good.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "EX009",
            "IDEA066",
            "REF015",
            "SRC001971",
            "SRC001972",
            "SRC001975",
            "SRC001987",
            "SRC002008"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC001971",
            "SRC001972",
            "SRC001975",
            "SRC001987",
            "SRC002008",
            "SRC001932",
            "SRC001937",
            "SRC001945"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA066"
          ],
          "example_refs": [
            "EX009"
          ],
          "reference_refs": [
            "REF015"
          ],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000011",
          "provider_handle": "p11"
        },
        {
          "text": "Worship is fellowship. God instituted it because he wants to be the center of our attention; he wants worship. This is the reason behind every true expression of it, whatever form that expression takes.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "IDEA067",
            "SRC001947",
            "SRC001951",
            "SRC001952"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC001947",
            "SRC001951",
            "SRC001952"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA067"
          ],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000012",
          "provider_handle": "p12"
        },
        {
          "text": "You can worship God simply by reading the Scriptures. There are moments when you are reading, and you become so engrossed in it, that the reading itself becomes an act of deep communion with him. This too is worship, even though no song is sung and no hand is lifted.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "IDEA068",
            "SRC001958",
            "SRC001961",
            "SRC001970"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC001958",
            "SRC001961",
            "SRC001970"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA068"
          ],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000013",
          "provider_handle": "p13"
        }
      ],
      "idea_refs": [
        "IDEA065",
        "IDEA066",
        "IDEA067",
        "IDEA068"
      ],
      "source_refs": [
        "SRC001921",
        "SRC001926",
        "SRC001930",
        "SRC001932",
        "SRC001937",
        "SRC001945",
        "SRC001971",
        "SRC001972",
        "SRC001975",
        "SRC001987",
        "SRC002008",
        "SRC001947",
        "SRC001951",
        "SRC001952",
        "SRC001958",
        "SRC001961",
        "SRC001970"
      ]
    },
    {
      "section_id": "SEC021",
      "title": "Nothing Added to What He Said",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "The kingdom of God is not in words. Jesus taught a simple way to receive power: you shall receive power when the Holy Spirit comes upon you. That reception was presented plainly, without elaborate human requirements attached to it.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "IDEA069",
            "SRC002038",
            "SRC002043",
            "SRC002064"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC002038",
            "SRC002043",
            "SRC002064"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA069"
          ],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000014",
          "provider_handle": "p14"
        },
        {
          "text": "I grew up in that kind of orthodoxy of the Pentecostal church, where it was taught that the gospel without power meant nothing. Yet I was not taught a simple way to get that power. Instead, I was told I had to fast for twenty-one days to release it. But did Jesus say that? Did he say, when you fast very well, then power is released? He did not say that.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "EX010",
            "IDEA070",
            "SRC002040",
            "SRC002052",
            "SRC002069",
            "SRC002073",
            "SRC002094"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC002040",
            "SRC002052",
            "SRC002069",
            "SRC002073",
            "SRC002094"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA070"
          ],
          "example_refs": [
            "EX010"
          ],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000015",
          "provider_handle": "p15"
        },
        {
          "text": "One apostle told people, if you want my power, if you want to get anointing like me, you must know the secret of midnight prayer. Is midnight prayer bad? No. But the question is whether this was a requirement Jesus himself ever stated, or a condition added afterward.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "IDEA070",
            "SRC002086",
            "SRC002089",
            "SRC002090"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC002086",
            "SRC002089",
            "SRC002090",
            "SRC002069",
            "SRC002073",
            "SRC002094"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA070"
          ],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [
            "UNC009"
          ],
          "paragraph_id": "P000016",
          "provider_handle": "p16"
        },
        {
          "text": "To add anything to what God has said that he did not say is an insult to him. Anytime you add something to his word, it stops that added teaching from working as it should; it does not allow it to work.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "IDEA071",
            "SRC002112",
            "SRC002114",
            "SRC002121"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC002112",
            "SRC002114",
            "SRC002121"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA071"
          ],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000017",
          "provider_handle": "p17"
        },
        {
          "text": "Notice something about ministers who teach a painful personal formula, saying, if you want to get anointing like me, you must do this exact grueling thing. You will not see a second person produced by that formula, because the principles they give did not originate from God in the first place. A method that truly comes from him reproduces itself in others; one that does not, stalls with the person who invented it.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "IDEA072",
            "SRC002130",
            "SRC002133",
            "SRC002139"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC002130",
            "SRC002133",
            "SRC002139"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA072"
          ],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000018",
          "provider_handle": "p18"
        },
        {
          "text": "Anything you practice repeatedly, you can master. As you practice something, you grow into it. But many people want power without practice; they want to experience it once, as a kind of magic moment, rather than growing into it through sustained, ordinary exercise.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "IDEA073",
            "SRC002174",
            "SRC002177",
            "SRC002183"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC002174",
            "SRC002177",
            "SRC002183"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA073"
          ],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000019",
          "provider_handle": "p19"
        }
      ],
      "idea_refs": [
        "IDEA069",
        "IDEA070",
        "IDEA071",
        "IDEA072",
        "IDEA073"
      ],
      "source_refs": [
        "SRC002038",
        "SRC002043",
        "SRC002064",
        "SRC002040",
        "SRC002052",
        "SRC002069",
        "SRC002073",
        "SRC002094",
        "SRC002086",
        "SRC002089",
        "SRC002090",
        "SRC002112",
        "SRC002114",
        "SRC002121",
        "SRC002130",
        "SRC002133",
        "SRC002139",
        "SRC002174",
        "SRC002177",
        "SRC002183"
      ]
    },
    {
      "section_id": "SEC022",
      "title": "What Fasting Is Actually For",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "When you fast, the body is brought under subjection, and the fruit of the Spirit — among it, temperance — is cultivated through that discipline. There is real spiritual profit in fasting. But this profit does not change God; it changes you.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "IDEA074",
            "SRC002199",
            "SRC002204",
            "SRC002213"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC002199",
            "SRC002204",
            "SRC002213"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA074"
          ],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000020",
          "provider_handle": "p20"
        },
        {
          "text": "Go read Isaiah 58. God said, when you fast, there is a profit in it. But that profit is what fasting does in you, not a shift it produces in him. There is never a divine condition attached to his blessing that fasting must first satisfy.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "IDEA075",
            "REF018",
            "SRC002228",
            "SRC002235",
            "SRC002236",
            "SRC002240"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC002228",
            "SRC002235",
            "SRC002236",
            "SRC002240"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA075"
          ],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [
            "REF018"
          ],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000021",
          "provider_handle": "p21"
        },
        {
          "text": "The disciples once were unable to cast out a demon, and at that point Jesus spoke about fasting and prayer. That's all we got from there — he did not establish a doctrine binding fasting and prayer as a universal rule for every deliverance. It was a word fitted to that particular situation, and it should not be stretched into a rigid, general formula.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "IDEA076",
            "SRC002249",
            "SRC002253",
            "SRC002257"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC002249",
            "SRC002253",
            "SRC002257"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA076"
          ],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000022",
          "provider_handle": "p22"
        },
        {
          "text": "In another encounter, Jesus asked the demon, what's your name? And it answered, my name is Legion. This exchange was tied to a specific prophecy in Genesis chapter 49, and a legion came to fulfill that prophecy. His question was not a general method for deliverance; it answered a particular prophetic purpose.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "EX011",
            "IDEA077",
            "REF019",
            "SRC002259",
            "SRC002264",
            "SRC002280",
            "SRC002283",
            "SRC002289"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC002259",
            "SRC002264",
            "SRC002280",
            "SRC002283",
            "SRC002289"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA077"
          ],
          "example_refs": [
            "EX011"
          ],
          "reference_refs": [
            "REF019"
          ],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000023",
          "provider_handle": "p23"
        },
        {
          "text": "By contrast, picture someone engaging a demon in prolonged conversation, as though it said, my name is Gombo, I came with a rachit, and drawing the exchange out at length. That kind of extended naming and conversing with demons is not the pattern Scripture gives us. Before you know it, you would be building an entire idol-worship system around a ritual that was never meant to be a method at all.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "EX012",
            "IDEA077",
            "SRC002293",
            "SRC002298",
            "SRC002304"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC002293",
            "SRC002298",
            "SRC002304",
            "SRC002259",
            "SRC002280",
            "SRC002289"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA077"
          ],
          "example_refs": [
            "EX012"
          ],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000024",
          "provider_handle": "p24"
        }
      ],
      "idea_refs": [
        "IDEA074",
